wolf3d-portable @PROJECT_VERSION@ - Linux direct-console host
=========================================================

This package runs directly on a Linux virtual console. It does not require
X11, Wayland, SDL, or a desktop environment. Video prefers DRM/KMS dumb
buffers and falls back to the Linux framebuffer, input uses evdev, and audio
uses ALSA.

Place original game data beside the executable or in nested subdirectories;
the launcher recursively finds complete sets without following symbolic links.
Alternatively, point to one exact directory:

  ./wolf3d --data /path/to/WL1

Names beginning with wolf prefer WL6 then WL1; the exact basename spear
prefers SOD then SDM, with cross-family fallback. Use -WL1/-WL6/-SDM/-SOD or
--game EXT for an exact set. SD1/SD2/SD3 always require explicit selection.

Optional defaults may be stored in wolf3d.conf beside this launcher, one
command-line option per line. An exact wolf3d-console.conf takes priority when
that binary name is used. Command-line arguments override config defaults.
Use --config FILE, --no-config, or --diag to select another file, bypass the
defaults, or print a data/hardware report without launching.

Run it from an active virtual console. The user needs permission to access the
DRM card, evdev input devices, and ALSA playback device; root normally has all
three. Typical non-root installations grant this through the distribution's
video, input, and audio groups or through seat/session device ACLs.

Host-specific options:

  --video auto|drm|fbdev
  --drm-device /dev/dri/card0
  --fb-device /dev/fb0
  --input-device /dev/input/event3    (repeatable)
  --alsa-device default
  --no-audio
  --help                              (also -h or /?)

Game audio options are --opl nuked|dbopl|silent and --sample-rate HZ. Only
drivers recorded in WOLF3D-LIB.txt are available. Emulated hardware is selected
independently with --adlib/-nosb, --pc-speaker/-noal, or --no-sound. Host-only
--no-audio suppresses ALSA output without changing detected game hardware.

Without explicit device options, the host selects the first connected DRM
display, falls back to $FRAMEBUFFER or /dev/fb0, opens usable evdev devices,
and uses the ALSA "default" PCM. Missing or unavailable audio produces a
warning and the game continues silently. --linux-console-help remains an
alias for --help.

The host preserves the engine's 320x200 indexed framebuffer and displays it
at the original monitor-intended 4:3 aspect ratio with letterboxing as needed.

libwolf3d.so is the generic engine library. When present, libNuked-OPL3.so is
kept separate and replaceable under the LGPL-2.1 license. System libdrm, ALSA,
libc, and their dependencies must be supplied by the target Linux installation.

See LICENSE.txt for wolf3d-portable's license, LICENSES/ for the explicit
wolf3d-lib and applicable component license texts, and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt for
component provenance.
